papers.relevance_to_N1:
This is the key prior work. Algorithm 1 is exactly 'evaluate or train on source with artificial random masking at a calibrated rate'. Theorem 5.2 plus Proposition 1 give the positive answer to N1's question under narrow conditions. Artificial dropout of a source reproduces the labeled target distribution, so it identifies the target risk of any model and therefore the ranking, when all of the following hold:
(i) P(X,Y) is invariant, meaning no population selection S;
(ii) the target mask is MCAR/UCAR, or v-MAR and simulated from P_t(M | X_c);
(iii) the target has at least as much missingness as the source (m^s <= m^t).
The eICU failure is itself evidence for N1's S-versus-M point. The authors explicitly say the hospitals differ by cohort selection rather than by missingness alone.

papers.overlap_with_candidate:
Already covered:
- the reduction to covariate shift when masks are observed and acquisition is MCAR or v-MAR, which makes importance weighting the standard remedy, so N1 must not present IW as new;
- 'dropout matches the target' as a formal identification result under UCAR;
- the dropout-as-regularization view (Theorem 4.1);
- one real natural-missingness test (eICU) that used target labels only for evaluation.
Not covered:
- model selection or ranking and risk differences between two models;
- a fully observed complete-case source whose cohort is itself selected (separating S from M);
- modality or block-level acquisition;
- MNAR or Y-dependent selective acquisition;
- sensitivity analysis or partial identification;
- confidence intervals on risk differences (only bootstrap CIs on metrics are reported);
- any check of whether artificial and natural missingness give different model rankings.

papers.method:
Reduce MNAR missingness shift to imputation: impute X_u separately in source and in target. When covariate shift is also present, then estimate importance weights P_T(Xo,Xu,R)/P_S(Xo,Xu,R) on the imputed data plus the missingness pattern (in practice an XGBoost domain classifier) and fit a reweighted outcome model. The paper compares 18 imputers:
- joint and z-model Bayesian latent-variable models, with and without 'leakage' of the target outcome;
- partial Bayesian models;
- MICE variants, including the random-indicator variant;
- PVAE, not-MIWAE and GINA.

papers.overlap_with_candidate:
Already covered:
- the statement that under MNAR missingness shift P(Y | X_obs, R) differs across domains, so IW on observed data fails. This is the ingredient of N1's minimal counterexample, though they do not frame it as a ranking reversal of dropout-based evaluation;
- an impute-then-reweight baseline for MNAR plus covariate shift;
- real eICU data with natural missingness and target labels available.
Not covered:
- model selection or risk differences;
- sensitivity-model bounds (odds-ratio or marginal-sensitivity type) on P_T(Y | x_obs, M);
- separating population selection S from mask M, since the invariant P(Xo,Xu) is assumed;
- artificial versus natural masking comparisons;
- modality-level acquisition.

Covers:
- the theoretical invariance/non-invariance dichotomy that N1's counterexample would rest on;
- an empirical demonstration that the ranking among missing-data methods changes under non-ignorable mask shift (observed-only/imputation versus mask-aware).
Does not cover:
- a fully observed source with artificial dropout;
- selection of the source population;
- natural acquisition data;
- risk-difference estimation, sensitivity bounds or uncertainty for choosing between two models.

Covers:
- acquisition-aware evaluation of multimodal models under MAR using plain IPW on both training and evaluation, which again means N1 cannot call IPW-based acquisition-aware evaluation new;
- the complete-case selection problem, which is close to N1's S;
- real natural-acquisition data.
Differs from N1:
- direction: the source is naturally missing and the target is hypothetically fully observed, whereas N1 has a fully observed source and a naturally missing target;
- no pairwise model-ranking or risk-difference inference;
- no MNAR sensitivity model;
- no comparison of artificial dropout versus natural missingness for model ranking.

refutations.residual_gap:
Not fully covered. The phenomenon is already documented, so it cannot be claimed as a finding. Artificial and natural missingness give different results or rankings in MedFuse App. A.4 vs A.6 (opposite AUROC direction), in CareBench and DrFuse (matched subset vs base cohort vs synthetic sweep), and in Groenwold's simulation. Qian et al. show that artificial-mask rankings depend on the masking protocol. Jeanselme et al. 2025 show model comparisons changing under a real, natural shift in the observation process. The explanatory hypothesis (cohort selection, label shift, prevalence) is written down in DAMS's eICU Discussion but left untested, and DAMS lists it as future work. The null-check tools are standard: case-mix membership models (Debray 2015), IPW to the eligible population, and prevalence reweighting.

After a serious search, I found no work that runs the controlled real-data audit C4 describes:
(i) one pre-registered eligible population and prediction cutoff, where record absence is separated from test-not-performed;
(ii) the same fixed trained models evaluated (a) on the fully observed subset with artificial modality dropout (MCAR and target-rate-calibrated) and (b) on the naturally masked full eligible cohort;
(iii) paired tests with bootstrap CIs on the sign of each pairwise risk difference;
(iv) a sequence of nulls applied BEFORE invoking the acquisition mechanism: reweight the complete subset to the eligible population on pre-acquisition covariates (S), adjust for label prevalence or label shift, and match mask rates. The claim would be that flips which survive all nulls are attributable to informative acquisition.

Novelty here is empirical and methodological rigor, not a new phenomenon or method. N1 must cite MedFuse, CareBench, DrFuse, DAMS, Qian et al. and Jeanselme et al. as prior evidence. Two cautions: the prevalence null must use prevalence-invariant metrics or explicit reweighting, because AUPRC and F1 shift mechanically with class balance. And if the flips vanish under the S and prevalence nulls, the result is a replication of DAMS's conjecture, not a new mechanism.

Confidence caveats: the WebSearch budget was exhausted, so this search used Europe PMC (including full-text queries), PubMed E-utilities, arXiv search, and direct PDF reads. Google Scholar, Semantic Scholar and OpenAlex were rate-limited, so a less-indexed clinical-epidemiology paper or conference abstract could have been missed.
